{{Source status|status=source-grounded architecture|note=Defines the V2 execution model that matures the existing MediaWiki, Scribunto, Semantic MediaWiki, Cargo, Book Matter, relationship, and BIThub/Discourse surfaces without treating speculative capabilities as deployed.}}

= Programmable knowledge substrate =

BITwiki is not only a collection of documents. It is a programmable knowledge substrate in which authored knowledge, structured state, deterministic computation, and multiple user/agent interfaces remain distinct but composable.

The central execution model is:

<pre>
authored wikitext / templates
          +
Semantic MediaWiki graph state
          +
Cargo operational state
          ↓
MediaWiki parser + Scribunto/Lua
          ↓
normalize → type-check → derive bounded rules → project → diagnose
          ↓
page / navigation / comparison / graph / maintenance / machine-facing views
          ↕
BIThub / Discourse integration layer
          ↕
humans + agents + workflows
</pre>

'''Lua is BITwiki's deterministic compiler/type-system/inference-and-projection layer.''' MediaWiki remains the execution substrate and page/revision system. Wikitext remains the primary authoring syntax. Semantic MediaWiki and Cargo remain structured state layers. BIThub/Discourse remains the interactive collaboration and workflow surface.

== Compiler is an architectural role ==

The word ''compiler'' does not mean Scribunto replaces MediaWiki's wikitext parser. MediaWiki parses wikitext and invokes modules. Lua then receives bounded inputs and wiki-resident structured data and performs deterministic transformations over them.

The useful compiler analogy is a sequence of passes:

# '''Normalize''' — canonicalize arguments, identifiers, lists, dates, units, labels, and other representations without changing their asserted meaning.
# '''Type-check''' — validate entity types, Domains, epistemic states, relationship shapes, required fields, and other governed contracts.
# '''Resolve''' — obtain relevant wiki-resident state through supported MediaWiki, Cargo, or Semantic MediaWiki interfaces.
# '''Derive''' — apply explicit bounded rules whose semantics are documented and safe for the relevant relation/data type.
# '''Project''' — choose and compose a view: relationship panel, breadcrumb, entity card, comparison, adjacency list, graph payload, maintenance panel, learning view, contribution quest, or other interface.
# '''Diagnose''' — expose malformed state, missing required knowledge, contradictions represented in structured state, dependency problems, stale mappings, or other actionable signals.

These passes may be implemented by multiple composable modules. The architecture is more important than any particular `Module:` title.

== Source of truth by layer ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Layer !! Owns !! Does not own
|-
| Authored pages / Book Matter || explanation, definitions, arguments, context, evidence-bearing prose, canonical reusable matter || implicit global inference
|-
| Semantic MediaWiki || durable typed page properties and graph relationships || arbitrary UI policy
|-
| Cargo || repeated structured operational records and queryable workflow state || canonical knowledge graph identity
|-
| Lua / Scribunto || deterministic normalization, validation, bounded inference, transformation, view models, rendering logic, diagnostics || autonomous state, external networking, hidden truth judgments
|-
| Templates || stable wikitext-facing invocation and declarative composition surfaces || deep duplicated procedural logic
|-
| MediaWiki || parsing, revisions, transclusion, namespaces, permissions, cache/execution environment, APIs || BITwiki-specific semantic policy by itself
|-
| BIThub / Discourse || conversation, coordination, active inference, contribution workflow, human/agent interaction || canonical durable knowledge merely because it appeared in a discussion
|-
| External agents / BITCORE workflows || research, synthesis, natural-language reasoning, external tools/APIs, proposed edits and structured findings || silent authority over canonical wiki state
|}

This separation prevents a view from becoming the database, a conversation from becoming canon, or a deterministic parser-time function from pretending to be an autonomous agent.

== BIThub / Discourse as the interaction plane ==

Many users and agents should not need to understand MediaWiki internals to interact with BITwiki's knowledge substrate.

BIThub provides the conversational and workflow-facing surface around the durable wiki. A request may begin as a discussion, research task, question, comparison, proposed relationship, missing-knowledge signal, or contribution workflow. Stable results can then enter the MediaWiki layer through explicit integration and review paths.

<pre>
human / agent
     ↓
BIThub topic, workflow, command, assistant or interface
     ↓
authenticated integration bridge
     ↓
MediaWiki API / page-template contracts / query surfaces
     ↓
SMW + Cargo + canonical page state
     ↓
Lua compiler/projection layer
     ↓
contextual view or diagnostic
     ↓
MediaWiki and/or BIThub presentation
</pre>

The '''integration bridge is not Scribunto'''. Lua executes inside MediaWiki's sandbox and should not be treated as the HTTP client, daemon, event consumer, queue worker, or secret-bearing transport between Discourse and MediaWiki. Transport belongs to explicit server-side integrations, plugins, APIs, workflows, or agents with appropriate authentication.

Lua's role is to make wiki state coherent and reusable once the request reaches the knowledge substrate, and to produce deterministic outputs that MediaWiki pages or BIThub integrations can consume.

== One state, many interfaces ==

A durable semantic assertion should normally be stored once and projected differently by context.

Example:

<pre>
Bitcoin Core → Implements → Bitcoin protocol
</pre>

Possible projections include:

* on the Bitcoin Core page: '''Implements → Bitcoin protocol''';
* on the Bitcoin protocol page: '''Implemented by ← Bitcoin Core''';
* in a graph: a directional edge;
* in BIThub: a compact entity/relationship card attached to a discussion;
* for an agent: a structured adjacency object;
* in maintenance: a validation warning if the relation violates a documented contract.

The inverse display does not require storing a second assertion. Presentation derivation is different from asserting a new fact.

== Inference policy ==

Lua may perform inference only when the rule is explicit, deterministic, bounded, and semantically justified.

Safe examples include:

* deriving an inverse '''display label''' from a stored directional edge;
* normalizing equivalent date/unit/string representations;
* identifying that a required field is absent;
* calculating structural completeness from explicitly defined criteria;
* identifying a dependency path for navigation or blast-radius inspection;
* generating a contribution task from an explicit missing-coverage condition.

Unsafe universal assumptions include:

* treating generic causality as transitive;
* manufacturing epistemic standing from page shape or source prestige;
* turning co-occurrence into a semantic relationship;
* silently persisting inferred facts as if they were authored assertions;
* resolving natural-language contradiction without an evidence-bearing external process.

Persistent derived assertions require an explicit provenance and governance policy. By default, Lua derivations are projections/diagnostics, not hidden writes to canonical state.

== Self-assembling pages ==

A rendered page can be understood as a composition rather than a monolithic document:

<pre>
canonical authored Book Matter
+ semantic identity
+ graph neighborhood
+ epistemic/provenance state
+ operational context where appropriate
+ deterministic Lua view rules
= rendered knowledge interface
</pre>

This supports context-sensitive infoboxes, related-page navigation, recursive taxonomy views, procedural comparisons, knowledge-health panels, graph projections, research tasks, learning paths, and other interfaces without duplicating the underlying knowledge.

The parent knowledge object must remain coherent and auditable. Dynamic composition must not make canonical authorship, provenance, or dependencies opaque.

== Agent-readable and interface-neutral knowledge ==

A Lua projection should not assume that its only consumer is the desktop article skin.

Where useful, the same normalized intermediate representation should support:

* human-readable MediaWiki rendering;
* compact BIThub/Discourse cards or embeds;
* API-oriented structured payloads generated by an integration layer;
* agent context assembly;
* graph visualization;
* maintenance/audit views;
* learning and contribution interfaces.

The preferred architecture is therefore:

<pre>
wiki state
   ↓
normalized view model
   ├── MediaWiki rendering
   ├── BIThub rendering
   ├── graph projection
   └── agent/machine projection
</pre>

Do not maintain separate semantic truth for each interface.

== Dogfooding the stack ==

BITwiki should be able to describe the systems that make BITwiki possible.

A material technology used by the platform should normally have two distinct forms when both are useful:

# a canonical Main-namespace knowledge object explaining what the technology is;
# a BITwiki project/architecture page explaining how BITwiki uses it, its runtime role, dependencies, constraints, and deployment consequences.

This applies progressively to languages, runtimes, applications, extensions, modules, scripts, databases, services, agents, protocols, APIs, daemons, kernels, and other consequential components.

The rule is not to manufacture pages for every transitive dependency. A component becomes first-class when understanding it materially improves operation, architecture, governance, contribution, debugging, provenance, or interoperability.

== Current implementation ==

The first deployed V2 Lua consumer remains `Template:Knowledge object` through `Module:Structure`. The implementation now treats `Module:Structure` as a public validation wrapper and delegates deterministic normalization/type checking to the `Module:BITwiki/*` compiler family.

The compiler-facing controlled vocabulary is source-controlled in `bitwiki-runtime-schema.json` and projected into `Module:BITwiki/Data/Schema` for `mw.loadData()` consumption. This is intended to prevent each module from inventing a private schema.

Cargo is already available to Lua through `mw.ext.cargo`. Native SMW objects through `mw.smw` require the SemanticScribunto capability and must not be assumed until the deployed extension/runtime combination supports it. Parser-function bridging can be used only where the trade-off is explicit.

== Security and execution boundary ==

Scribunto is intentionally sandboxed and resource-bounded. The compiler layer must therefore remain parse-time deterministic computation over permitted wiki state.

It is not the place for:

* filesystem access;
* arbitrary network requests;
* long-running/background computation;
* secret management;
* autonomous scheduling;
* persistent private memory;
* unrestricted process execution;
* LLM inference.

Those responsibilities belong to MediaWiki/Discourse extensions, APIs, backend services, BIThub workflows, BITCORE agents, or other explicitly governed infrastructure.

== Design test ==

Before adding Lua, ask:

# Is the logic reusable and non-trivial?
# Does it transform or validate existing state rather than invent a second source of truth?
# Is the rule deterministic and safe at parse time?
# Does more than one interface or consumer benefit from a normalized representation?
# Can the behavior be tested independently of presentation?
# Would keeping the logic in wikitext create duplication, parser-function complexity, or semantic drift?

If not, a template, query, property, authored page, or external workflow may be the better primitive.

